  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          A city with one centre can be judged on a radius. Barcelona cannot,
          because it has never had one. The old city is a knot of medieval lanes
          wedged between the Rambla, Via Laietana and the water. The Eixample is
          the nineteenth-century grid laid across the plain above it, holding
          most of the architecture people come for. Montjuïc rises at the
          south-western corner. Gràcia — an independent town until 1897 — sits
          above the Diagonal keeping its own squares and its own hours. And
          along the eastern edge runs several kilometres of beach that is not a
          resort strip bolted onto the city but a working part of it. No address
          is near all five, and the argument that settles
          <a href="/best-areas-to-stay-in-seville/">where to sleep in a compact
          city like Seville</a> — where everything worth seeing stands inside a
          two-kilometre oval — simply does not transfer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The metro is excellent, and it makes this sound like a solved problem.
          It is not, quite. Two lines do nearly all the work a visitor needs. L3
          threads Barcelona Sants to the foot of Montjuïc at Poble Sec and
          Paral·lel, into the old city at Drassanes and Liceu, up through
          Catalunya, Passeig de Gràcia and Diagonal, and out at Fontana and
          Lesseps in Gràcia. L4 runs the other diagonal entirely — Poblenou,
          Llacuna, Bogatell, Barceloneta, Jaume I at the edge of the Born,
          Urquinaona, Passeig de Gràcia, Girona, Verdaguer, and Joanic in
          Gràcia. Between them they touch every area on this page, which is why
          the honest measure of a Barcelona neighbourhood is not how central it
          is but whether it sits on a spine, and which one.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the spines do not do is run all night. Transports Metropolitans
          de Barcelona has the metro finishing at midnight from Sunday to
          Thursday, at two in the morning on Fridays and the nights before
          public holidays, and running continuously through Saturday night into
          Sunday. After that the night buses take over, and every NitBus line
          but one stops at Plaça de Catalunya, because the night network is a
          wheel with a single hub. So lateness has a geography of its own,
          measured not in the distance from your room to the bar but in how far
          your bed lies from one square. That, and street noise, and slope, and
          whether a vehicle can reach your door with a suitcase in it, is what
          actually varies across the seven areas below.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

