      <p>
        That you will walk and use public transport rather than drive, because
        parking a car here is a separate and worse problem. That you have
        between two and seven nights. That there is no universally best
        district, only a best answer to your particular week. That the street
        matters as much as the district, and the room orientation sometimes
        more than either. That is why no hotel is named on this page: the
        building you book will have changed hands, name and price by the time
        you read this, and the street outside it will not have changed at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing here is documented rather than reasoned. A six-night stay
        recorded on this site as
        <a href="/7-days-in-barcelona/">seven days in Barcelona read through
        Gaudí</a> was taken on Carrer Sant Pau in the Raval — which is not one
        of the seven areas below, and the distinction is worth making rather
        than blurring. What it supplies is first-hand knowledge of sleeping a
        hundred metres off the Rambla in the old city, and of leaving it by
        pre-booked taxi at half past four in the morning through lanes that were
        finally empty. It licenses no opinion about waking up in Gràcia or
        Poblenou. Those verdicts are assessed — from the city's own district
        boundaries, from published transport, and from walking the same streets
        across two August visits. Where a judgement is first-hand, it says so.
      </p>
    </div>

