  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="eixample">Eixample — the unglamorous right answer</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Ildefons Cerdà laid the Eixample out in the 1860s across the plain
        between the demolished city walls and the villages above, and the plan
        still does what it was designed to do: move people and air. The streets
        are wide, straight and counted in blocks; every crossing opens into the
        chamfered octagonal corner that is the grid's signature; the buildings
        are tall, solid and largely nineteenth-century, with a courtyard buried
        in the middle of each block. Officially the district holds six
        neighbourhoods. Travellers mean two — the
        <strong>Dreta de l'Eixample</strong> east of Balmes, which carries
        Passeig de Gràcia and its Gaudí houses and runs up towards the Sagrada
        Família, and <strong>l'Antiga Esquerra</strong> west of it, which is
        quieter, more residential and costs less. The difference between them is
        mostly price and calm rather than convenience.
      </p>
      <p>
        The convenience argument is the strongest on this page and worth
        stating precisely. Passeig de Gràcia is the only station in Barcelona
        served by L2, L3 and L4 at once — which is to say, it is where both
        metro spines cross — and the airport train from Terminal 2 stops there
        as well. From a room within ten minutes' walk of it you reach the old
        city, Gràcia, the beach, the foot of Montjuïc, Barcelona Sants and the
        airport without ever having to plan a route. Nowhere else here offers
        that. It is why the Eixample is the safest recommendation for a first
        visit, and equally why it stops being necessary once you know the city
        well enough not to need every option at once.
      </p>
      <p>
        What you pay is exposure. The width that disperses noise also admits
        traffic, and some of these streets carry a great deal of it: Gran Via,
        Aragó and Balmes are through-routes rather than addresses, and a room
        facing one of them faces four lanes. This is where the Eixample's one
        genuinely important piece of advice lives, and it concerns the floor
        plan rather than the district. Flats here have exterior rooms facing the
        street and interior rooms facing the block's courtyard. The interior
        room is darker and often has no view at all; it is also markedly
        quieter, and in July cooler. If you sleep badly, the courtyard side of a
        building on a busy Eixample street will serve you better than the street
        side of a building on a quiet one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other cost is atmosphere, and it should not be oversold as a
        tragedy: you step out onto a broad pavement with plane trees, a pharmacy
        and a bank — the ordinary machinery of a large European city rather than
        anything you want to photograph. Against that, arriving here is easy in
        a way it is nowhere in the old city, with vehicles reaching every door
        and lifts as standard. One caveat: two of the nine metro stations TMB
        lists as not yet adapted for reduced mobility sit on this ground,
        <strong>Urquinaona</strong> and <strong>Verdaguer</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stay here if</strong> it is your first visit, you want maximum
        optionality, you are arriving with luggage or leaving on an early
        flight, or someone in the party sleeps badly and you are willing to book
        a courtyard room. <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong> you want to walk
        out of the door into something older than 1870, or you are staying a
        week and would rather have a neighbourhood than a grid.
        <strong>The trade-off:</strong> you buy the best-connected address in
        Barcelona and pay for it in traffic and in charm.
      </p>
    </div>

