    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="gotic">Barri Gòtic — the most atmospheric bed and the least sleep</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The Gothic Quarter occupies the oldest ground in Barcelona, between the
        Rambla and Via Laietana, with the Cathedral at its heart and fragments
        of the Roman wall still standing inside later buildings. Its lanes are
        three or four metres across in places, paved rather than kerbed, walled
        in stone five or six storeys high, and they open without warning into
        small squares — Plaça del Pi, Plaça de Sant Felip Neri, the arcaded
        Plaça Reial. Nothing else in this article is as good to walk through at
        eight in the morning. Nothing else is as hard to sleep in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reason is acoustic, and structural rather than moral. A lane four
        metres wide between two stone faces is a chamber: sound does not
        disperse, it bounces, and a conversation at ground level arrives at a
        second-floor window with very little lost. Add the density of bars, a
        pedestrian flow that thins but does not stop between one and three in
        the morning, and the plain fact that Barcelona eats late and drinks
        outdoors, and you have a district where the difference between a good
        night and a bad one is not the quarter at all but the specific block —
        and, again, whether the room faces the lane or a light well. A listing
        that says only "Gothic Quarter" is telling you nothing you can act on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two frictions compound it. The first is luggage: a great many of these
        lanes are bollarded or pedestrianised, so a taxi sets you down on the
        perimeter — Via Laietana, the Rambla, Plaça Nova — and the last hundred
        to three hundred metres are yours, over uneven paving and quite possibly
        with a step at the door. The second is the building stock. These are
        old, narrow, deep buildings: rooms are small, lifts are not guaranteed
        and where they exist they are often cupboard-sized. Against all of
        which, the quarter is well served by metro — Jaume I on L4, Liceu and
        Drassanes on L3, Catalunya at the top — and you are already standing in
        the middle of what most first-time visitors came for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stay here if</strong> you have two or three nights, travel
        light, sleep through noise, and want the shortest possible distance
        between the door and the old city. <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong>
        you are a light sleeper, have small children, have any mobility
        constraint, are arriving late with a large case, or are staying more
        than three nights. <strong>The trade-off:</strong> the most atmospheric
        address in Barcelona, bought with your sleep and paid for again every
        time you arrive or leave with a bag.
      </p>
    </div>

