    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="born">El Born — the best dinner, and the bill after midnight</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        El Born is not, strictly, a neighbourhood at all. The city's official map
        of seventy-three barris puts it inside <strong>Sant Pere, Santa Caterina
        i la Ribera</strong>, and what people mean by the name is the eastern
        end of that: the ground between Via Laietana and the Parc de la
        Ciutadella, gathered around the great bare Catalan Gothic nave of Santa
        Maria del Mar and the promenade of the Passeig del Born behind it. The
        distinction matters when you book, because the same official
        neighbourhood also contains streets up by Sant Pere that are quiet,
        residential and cheaper — and they are not what anyone is selling when
        they say Born.
      </p>
      <p>
        What the Born does better than anywhere else in this article is dinner.
        The lanes off the Passeig del Born and around Carrer de Montcada carry
        the densest concentration of good, small, seriously run restaurants and
        wine bars in the old city, and unlike the Gothic Quarter across Via
        Laietana the trade is not overwhelmingly for visitors. You can leave the
        door at half past nine with no plan and eat properly within four
        minutes. That is most of the case for staying here.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost arrives at closing time, and it varies by block more sharply
        than anywhere else on this page. The streets immediately around the
        Passeig del Born and Carrer del Rec are a going concern until well after
        midnight; three turnings north or east, towards the Ciutadella or up
        past the Mercat de Santa Caterina, the same official neighbourhood is
        genuinely quiet. The lanes are medieval in width and behave acoustically
        like the Gòtic's. Taxi access is better — Passeig de Picasso, Via
        Laietana and the Passeig del Born itself all take vehicles — but the
        interior lanes do not. Jaume I on L4 sits on the western edge, Arc de
        Triomf on L1 at the top, and the park is on the doorstep, which is the
        closest thing the old city has to a garden.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stay here if</strong> you have two to four nights, eating is a
        substantial part of why you came, you want the old city without the full
        saturation of the Gòtic, and you will look at the exact street on a map
        first. <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong> you are staying a week, need a
        lift and a large room, or expect to be asleep by eleven at a weekend.
        <strong>The trade-off:</strong> the best evening in Barcelona, and an
        even chance of having booked a bedroom directly above it.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="gracia">Gràcia — the one that feels inhabited</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Gràcia was a separate town until Barcelona annexed it in 1897, and it
        has never entirely stopped behaving like one. The history is legible in
        the street plan: a grid, but tighter and older than the Eixample's, of
        low buildings and narrow roads opening into a series of proper squares —
        Plaça de la Vila de Gràcia under its clock tower, Plaça del Sol, Plaça
        de la Virreina, Plaça de la Revolució. The squares are the point. They
        are not ornamental spaces that visitors photograph; they are where the
        surrounding streets eat, drink, hold their meetings and let their
        children run about, and an evening in one of them is the least performed
        hour available to a traveller in this city. The Mercat de la Llibertat,
        an iron hall of 1888 decorated by Gaudí's collaborator Francesc
        Berenguer, still does the neighbourhood's shopping.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be precise about which Gràcia you are booking. The district contains
        five official neighbourhoods; the one travellers mean is
        <strong>Vila de Gràcia</strong>, between the Diagonal and the Travessera
        de Dalt. Above and around it, Vallcarca, El Coll and La Salut are
        further out, steeper and considerably less useful as a base, whatever a
        listing says about being in Gràcia.
      </p>
      <p>
        The transport is good, and it is the far end of both spines, which is a
        genuine cost rather than a quibble. L3 reaches Fontana and Lesseps, L4
        reaches Joanic, and the Ferrocarrils station at Gràcia adds a third
        option. From Fontana you are perhaps fifteen minutes from Passeig de
        Gràcia, twenty-five to thirty from the old city, and further still from
        the beach, which needs a change. Add the slope — the ground rises
        steadily northwards, and Lesseps sits noticeably higher than Diagonal —
        and Gràcia becomes an area to arrive at in a vehicle rather than on foot
        from a station with a case in hand. It is also where the night-bus
        geometry bites hardest: once the metro stops, getting home means Plaça
        de Catalunya and a transfer, or a fare.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of that matters much over five nights and it matters a great deal
        over two, which makes this the clearest length-of-stay argument on the
        page. On a short trip, twenty-five minutes each way is a meaningful
        fraction of your waking time; on a longer one it becomes the price of
        living somewhere real, and you stop noticing it by the third morning.
        One seasonal warning: the <strong>Festa Major de Gràcia</strong> in
        August fills the decorated streets for a week, which is either the best
        reason to be here or a decisive one not to be, and the squares are loud
        until very late throughout.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stay here if</strong> you have five nights or more, have been to
        Barcelona before, want a neighbourhood rather than a district, or would
        simply rather eat where the people at the next table live.
        <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong> you have two nights, the beach is a
        daily fixture, or hills and stairs are a problem.
        <strong>The trade-off:</strong> you spend roughly forty minutes a day
        getting in and out, and are repaid in squares, in prices, and in the
        distinct sense of being somebody's neighbour rather than somebody's
        guest.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
