    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="sant-antoni">Sant Antoni and Poble-sec — two neighbourhoods, one avenue apart</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        These are routinely sold as a single area, and they are not one. The
        city's own boundaries put <strong>Sant Antoni</strong> in the Eixample
        district and <strong>Poble-sec</strong> in Sants-Montjuïc, with Avinguda
        del Paral·lel between them, and the two feel as different as that
        suggests. Sant Antoni is Cerdà's grid continued — wide streets,
        chamfered corners, tall regular blocks. Poble-sec is older, denser and
        lower, built up the first slope of Montjuïc in narrow streets that were
        housing working Barcelona long before the Eixample reached them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sant Antoni's centre of gravity is its market, and the market explains
        the neighbourhood. Cerdà planned it into the grid; Antoni Rovira i Trias
        built it in iron between 1872 and 1882 as the first market outside the
        demolished walls, four great arms in the shape of a cross under an
        octagonal dome twenty-eight metres high. It closed in 2009 and reopened
        in May 2018 after a refurbishment that turned up the old Sant Antoni
        bastion of the city wall, now visitable on the floor below. It is three
        markets in one — the food hall, the Encants, and the Sunday book and
        collectors' fair that has run since the 1930s — and around it the
        streets have filled with the small, confident restaurants and vermouth
        bars that follow a restored market. Carrer del Parlament is the spine of
        that.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a caveat here with a date on it. Barcelona City Council began
        work on 25 June 2026 to make the neighbourhood's traffic-calmed layout
        permanent, on <strong>Carrer del Comte Borrell</strong> between Manso
        and the Paral·lel and on <strong>Carrer del Parlament</strong> between
        Viladomat and the Ronda de Sant Pau, together with the new square at
        their junction. The council states plainly that while the work is on
        there will be disruption to circulation and to the use of public space,
        and that it should be finished in June 2027. The result — kerbless,
        wider, greener streets with priority given to pedestrians — will be
        better than what was there before. Until then, the heart of Sant
        Antoni's eating quarter is a construction site, which is a real
        consideration for anyone booking on those two streets this year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Poble-sec across the avenue is the cheaper and livelier half after dark.
        Carrer de Blai is a pedestrian street of pintxo bars that fills from
        eight and stays full; the streets climbing off it — Blesa, Poeta
        Cabanyes — are steep, residential and quiet by comparison. Montjuïc
        begins at the top: the funicular runs from Paral·lel, and the museums,
        gardens and the long view over the port are twenty minutes from the door
        rather than a half-day expedition. Transport is good and slightly
        awkward — Sant Antoni sits on L2 alone, Poble Sec and Paral·lel on L3,
        with Paral·lel the interchange — and Plaça d'Espanya is a short walk,
        which also puts the Ferrocarrils line out to
        <a href="/1-day-in-colonia-guell/">Gaudí's crypt at Colònia Güell</a> on
        the doorstep. What you do not get, in either half, is a postcard when
        you open the door.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stay here if</strong> you have been to Barcelona before, are
        staying four nights or more, want proper value, and care more about
        eating and drinking well among locals than about the view from the
        window. <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong> this is a short first visit
        and you would resent the fifteen minutes into the old city, or if you
        were about to book Carrer del Parlament itself before mid-2027.
        <strong>The trade-off:</strong> the best price-to-quality ratio in
        central Barcelona, bought by giving up the scenery outside the door.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mercat-de-sant-antoni-lantern.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/mercat-de-sant-antoni-lantern-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/mercat-de-sant-antoni-lantern-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/mercat-de-sant-antoni-lantern-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/mercat-de-sant-antoni-lantern-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/mercat-de-sant-antoni-lantern.jpg 2048w`}
