      caption="Twenty-eight metres of iron lantern over the crossing of four arms — the first market Barcelona built outside its walls, shut for nine years and reopened in 2018. Neighbourhoods reorganise themselves around a restored market, and this one did."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="poblenou">Poblenou — space, and the beach without the crush</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Poblenou is the part of Barcelona that was its factories. Textile mills
        and foundries covered this ground so thoroughly that it was called the
        Catalan Manchester; the 1992 Olympics cleared the seafront, and the 22@
        programme has spent two decades filling the interior with offices,
        studios and new flats among the surviving brick chimneys. The result is
        the least old-feeling and most genuinely comfortable base on this page.
        Blocks are larger, buildings are newer, lifts and air conditioning are
        normal, and a given budget buys appreciably more floor space than
        anywhere between here and Montjuïc.
      </p>
      <p>
        Be careful with the name, because the official map is unusually helpful
        here. <strong>El Poblenou</strong> and <strong>la Vila Olímpica del
        Poblenou</strong> are two separate neighbourhoods, and the difference is
        exactly the one you care about. The Vila Olímpica is the Olympic rebuild
        around Port Olímpic — the marina, the towers, the clubs — a nightlife
        destination with the noise profile to match. El Poblenou proper lies
        inland and north-east of it, and its axis is the <strong>Rambla del
        Poblenou</strong>: a genuine neighbourhood promenade of low buildings,
        benches under the plane trees, and almost nobody carrying a map. If a
        listing says Poblenou, establish which of the two it stands in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The beaches are the other half of the case. Nova Icària, Bogatell and
        Mar Bella run north-east from Port Olímpic, and they are wider, better
        spaced and considerably calmer than Barceloneta — the same water, a
        fraction of the density, and a five-to-ten-minute walk from most of the
        neighbourhood. For a family in August that is not a nuance; it is the
        difference between a holiday and a daily negotiation. Transport is
        straightforward: Llacuna, Poblenou, Bogatell and Selva de Mar sit on the
        L4 spine, with the T4 tram alongside, which puts the old city fifteen to
        twenty minutes away without a change. Those minutes are the entire price
        of the area, and they are charged twice a day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stay here if</strong> you are travelling with children, staying
        five nights or more, coming in summer, want a real apartment rather than
        a small room, or have been to Barcelona already and do not need the
        Gothic Quarter outside the window. <strong>Look elsewhere if</strong>
        you have two or three nights and a first-visit checklist, or if evenings
        on foot among old stone are the reason you came.
        <strong>The trade-off:</strong> the most comfortable rooms and the
        calmest sand in Barcelona, at the cost of never being able to walk home
        from dinner in the old city.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/beach-towards-port-olimpic.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/beach-towards-port-olimpic-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/beach-towards-port-olimpic-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/beach-towards-port-olimpic-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/beach-towards-port-olimpic-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/beach-towards-port-olimpic.jpg 2048w`}
